proofs.proof_tex:
Fix a cohort \(s\), an admissible query \(z_0\), and
\(\xi\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}(z_0)\).  All probabilities below are under \(\mathbb K_\xi\).  Since \(0<\lambda\le1\), the order convention gives \(r=0\), so \(G_{sT}(z_{0s},b)\) is scalar.  Continuity and local positivity of \(K\) give constants \(k_0>0\) and \(\eta\in(0,\delta_0]\) such that \(K(v)\ge k_0\) for \(\|v\|_2\le\eta\).  The ball \(B(z_{0s},\eta b)\) is inside the cube for \(b\le1\).  If \(v_d\) is the unit-ball volume, the query-conditional density lower bound gives, uniformly in \(t\),
\[
 p_{tb}:=\Pr\{\|X_{st}-z_{0s}\|_2\le\eta b\}
 \ge c_Xv_d\eta^db^d=:c_pb^d.                                \tag{1}
\]
If \(M_b\) counts these visits, then
\[
 G_{sT}(z_{0s},b)\ge\frac{k_0M_b}{Tb^d}.                      \tag{2}
\]

Set
\[
 a_T=\left\lceil\frac{3\log(T\vee2)}{|\log\rho_0|}\right\rceil.
\]
Partition the calendar into consecutive blocks of length \(a_T\), and within each parity retain alternate complete blocks.  Consecutive retained blocks are separated by a complete block.  The definition of beta mixing and iterative replacement by product marginals bound, for each parity, the total variation between the retained-block joint law and the product of its block marginals by
\[
 \left\lceil\frac{T}{a_T}\right\rceil\beta_0\rho_0^{a_T}
 \le CT^{-2}.                                                  \tag{3}
\]
Adding the two parity errors preserves the order \(CT^{-2}\).  This comparison concerns only the design blocks and asserts no response-block independence.

For one independent comparison block let \(Y_j\in[0,a_T]\) count its visits.  Convexity places \(e^{-y/a_T}\) below the chord joining its endpoint values, so (1) gives
\[
 \mathbb Ee^{-Y_j/a_T}
 \le1-\frac{1-e^{-1}}{a_T}\mathbb EY_j
 \le\exp(-cb^d).                                              \tag{4}
\]
Exponential Markov applied under the product-block law, followed by (3) and addition of the two parities, yields constants \(c_1,C_1>0\) such that
\[
 \Pr\{M_b\le c_1Tb^d\}
 \le C_1\exp(-c_1Tb^d/a_T)+C_1T^{-2}                         \tag{5}
\]
when \(Tb^d\ge C_1a_T\); enlarging \(C_1\) makes the bound trivial below that scale.  Compatibility of \(c_G\) with the population local-constant Gram bound makes (2) at least \(c_G\) off the event in (5).

Put
\[
 b_\circ=(Tn_s)^{-1/(2\lambda+d)}\vee N^{-1/2}
          \vee(a_T/T)^{1/d}.                                  \tag{6}
\]
The smallest grid point not below a number in \([b_{0s},1]\) is at most twice that number.  Apply (5) at dyadic candidates of order \(2^jb_\circ\).  Apply the design-block total-variation comparison once to the union of all bad-scale events before taking the product-law union bound; hence its \(CT^{-2}\) error is charged only once.  The exponential tails imply
\[
 \Pr\{h_s^\star>C2^jb_\circ\}
 \le C\exp(-c2^{jd})+CT^{-2},\qquad j\ge0,                   \tag{7}
\]
until the scale reaches one, after which the probability is zero.  Dyadic tail summation, again charging the common comparison failure only once, gives
\[
 \mathbb E(h_s^\star)^{2\lambda}
 \le Cb_\circ^{2\lambda}+CT^{-2}.                            \tag{8}
\]
If \(b_\circ\) is bounded away from zero, (8) follows directly from \(h_s^\star\le1\).  Since \(a_T\le C\log(T\vee2)\) and \(2\lambda/d\le2\), (6)--(8) imply
\[
 \mathbb E(h_s^\star)^{2\lambda}
 \le C\left\{(Tn_s)^{-2\lambda/(2\lambda+d)}+N^{-\lambda}
 +\left(\frac{\log(T\vee2)}{T}\right)^{2\lambda/d}\right\}. \tag{9}
\]
Cauchy--Schwarz gives the asserted first-moment bound for \((h_s^\star)^\lambda\).

At \(b=1\), (1)--(5) imply
\(\Pr\{G_{sT}(z_{0s},1)<c_G\}\le CT^{-2}\).  If this event fails for every cohort, \((z_{0s},1)\) belongs to every fold--cohort anchor set, so fallback is impossible.  A union bound over fixed \(S\) gives
\[
 \sup_{\xi\in\mathcal M_{N,T,\mathbf n}(z_0)}
 \Pr_{\mathbb K_\xi}(\mathsf F_{\mathrm{fb}})\le CT^{-2}.  \tag{10}
\]
All constants are uniform because the fixed-query density and mixing bounds are uniform.  In the independent-calendar subclass take \(a_T=1\) and omit (3), which replaces the last term in (9) by \(T^{-2\lambda/d}\).  Lemma \texttt{lem:panel-fixed-query-kernel-transfer} applies these conclusions to each panel law for marginal-almost-every admissible query.
